=== Duplicants & Labor

*Duplicant (dupe):* The colonists of the asteroid, printed three at a time from the Printing Pod. Each has their own needs — oxygen, food, sleep and morale — and their own mix of attributes, interests and skills.

*Morale:* A dupe's contentment, raised by food quality, decor, sleep, downtime and a few building bonuses. It is the budget you pay for skill points with: every skill point spent raises a dupe's morale *expectation*, and if expectation ever exceeds morale the dupe stresses, vomits and eventually melts down.

*Skill points:* Earned when a dupe gains experience and spent on the skill tree. Points are permanent — each one makes a dupe better at something but also raises their morale expectation.

*Priorities:* The schedule of what a dupe does first, set on their personal priority grid and nudged by the colony-wide task priorities. Two dupes with different priorities turn "the colony needs help" into "the digger digs and the cook cooks."

*Job assignment:* The Jobs tab, where dupes are assigned to job paths — digger, builder, researcher, rancher and so on. Each path is a ladder of skill tiers that unlocks that job's abilities.

*Attribute / interest:* Attributes are the raw stats — digging, building, strength, athletics and the rest — that measure how well a dupe performs a task. Interests mark the tasks a dupe enjoys: a dupe with an interest in a skill gains experience in it much faster, which is why specialists are built from interests, not wishes.

=== Oxygen & Air

*Oxygen diffuser:* The colony's first oxygen machine — feed it algae and power and it converts the algae into breathable oxygen. It gives off a little heat while it runs and needs a dupe to refill it.

*Electrolyzer:* Splits water into oxygen and hydrogen using power, and outputs both hot. It is the mid-game upgrade to the diffuser: the oxygen keeps the base breathing and the hydrogen is fuel waiting for a generator.

*SPOM:* Self-Powered Oxygen Module — an electrolyzer room wired so its own hydrogen feeds a hydrogen generator, which powers the electrolyzer. Once started, it produces oxygen that is nearly free, and it usually includes a smart battery and a hydro sensor so it runs only when needed.

*Algae:* The green biomass of the slime biomes — the diffuser's fuel and the early colony's oxygen reserve. It is also critter food: shine bugs eat it, and pacu will happily take it.

*Polluted oxygen:* The foul, germ-laden air that seeps from polluted water and slime. Breathing it risks sickness, but a puft considers it dinner.

*Carbon dioxide:* The heavy gas that sinks — produced by breathing, coal generators and other combustion. It pools in the bottom of the base, where it chokes dupes but feeds slicksters.

*Oxygen mask / atmo suit / exosuit:* Suit clothing that carries its own air supply so a dupe can work in bad atmospheres. The oxygen mask is the cheap early version; the atmo suit — renamed the exosuit in Spaced Out — is the full suit that protects against most hazards and must be refilled at a dock.

*Atmo suit dock:* The building that stores suits and fills them with oxygen. Dupes suit up at the dock on their way out of the base and drop their suits back off on return.

=== Food & Water

*Mealwood:* The first farmable food plant — grows meal lice from dirt with no water and tolerates the early base's temperature. It is the colony's food staple until something better shows up.

*Bristle blossom:* A plant that turns water and light into bristle berries. It needs irrigation and decent temperature, but the berries are a real step up in food quality from meal lice.

*Cooking:* The kitchen step that turns raw ingredients into proper meals — better food means higher quality, and higher quality means more morale. It is the difference between eating raw meal lice and eating cooked food.

*Grill:* The Electric Grill, the standard kitchen building that cooks raw ingredients into meals. It is the first place a colony does its cooking.

*Food spoilage:* Food rots over time and eventually turns to rot, which is inedible. Cold storage, a fridge, or simply keeping food in carbon dioxide slows rot dramatically.

*Water sieve:* Filters polluted water into clean water, leaving polluted dirt behind. It needs a filtration medium — sand or regolith — and is the machine that closes the water loop.

*Liquid reservoir:* A tank that stores liquids. Beyond simple storage, a reservoir placed in a pipe line breaks the loop, so a pipe that would otherwise dead-end has somewhere to put its contents.

=== Power & Heat

*Power grid:* The network of wires and generators that carries electricity around the base. Every wire has a wattage limit, and drawing more than the wire is rated for damages it and starts fires.

*Heavi-watt wire:* The industrial-strength wire with a huge capacity — it carries almost any load, at the cost of being ugly, blocking movement, and leaking a fair amount of heat.

*Battery:* Stores surplus power so generators can rest when nothing is drawing. Batteries slowly lose stored power to runoff and give off heat; the smart battery adds an automation output that reports its charge.

*Hamster wheel:* The manual generator — a dupe runs in place to produce power. It is the colony's first source of electricity and its least efficient.

*Coal generator:* The first automatic generator — burns coal for power, giving off carbon dioxide and heat. It is reliable and loud and needs a steady coal supply.

*Hydrogen generator:* Burns hydrogen for clean power, returning a little water. It is the generator inside a SPOM, and its only real job is turning the electrolyzer's waste hydrogen into electricity.

*Steam turbine:* The heat deletion machine — built above a sealed chamber, it pulls in hot steam, turns its heat into power, and spits out cooler water. It requires plastic to build.

*Aquatuner:* Cools liquid piped through it by a significant amount, but dumps the heat it removes straight into itself and its surroundings. An unmanaged aquatuner melts its own room; pair it with a steam chamber.

*Thermo regulator:* The gas version of the aquatuner — cools gas piped through it and dumps the heat into itself. Same principle, same heat-dump problem, for gas loops instead of liquid ones.

*Heat deletion:* The act of removing heat from the map for good. In the base game the steam turbine is the real one: heat enters as steam, the turbine consumes it, and the mass leaves cooler than it arrived.

*Thermal mass:* How much heat an object can absorb before its temperature moves. High thermal mass is a buffer — a big pool of water heats up slowly and cools slowly, buying time against heat creep.

*Insulated tile:* A wall with almost no thermal conductivity — heat barely crosses it. It is the first line of heat defense and the standard wall for hot machinery and geyser chambers.

*Liquid lock:* A pool of liquid across an opening that blocks gas from passing while dupes walk right through. It is the cheap airlock for keeping atmospheres separate without a door.

=== Critters

*Hatch:* The starting ranch animal — eats minerals, poops coal, and eventually contributes meat and eggs. Rocks in, fuel and food out.

*Drecko:* The tree-dwelling reptile of the surface biome — eats mealwood (and later balm lily) and is sheared for reed fiber, the material for insulation and suits, along with phosphorite.

*Puft:* The pollution recycler — eats polluted oxygen and excretes slime, turning the foul air around a polluted water pool into a resource you actually want.

*Slickster:* The oil biome's answer to carbon dioxide — eats CO2 and excretes crude oil. Park a few in the colony's CO2 pit and the bottom of the base becomes a slow oil well.

*Pip:* The tree rat — eats plants, buries seeds, and by doing so plants wild plants wherever it walks. It also poops dirt.

*Shine bug:* The glow critter of the starting biome — eats algae and emits light. A shine bug in a living room is free lighting and free morale as long as it is fed.

*Ranching:* Raising critters in a ranch for their eggs, meat and products. The husbandry skill tree unlocks grooming, shearing and the buildings that keep a stable productive.

*Stable:* The room designation for a ranch — a properly enclosed, sized and furnished room where dupes care for critters and the animals stay put.

=== Geysers & Terrain

*Geyser:* A renewable source buried in the asteroid that erupts a fixed resource — water, steam, natural gas, and more — on a schedule of active and dormant periods. Tapping one turns "survive" into "thrive", because it adds mass from nothing.

*Vent:* A geyser that erupts a gas rather than a liquid — natural gas, chlorine, hydrogen, carbon dioxide.

*Cool steam vent:* Erupts steam at a relatively tame temperature — cool by geyser standards, hence the name, though it still needs careful handling. It is one of the favorite early water sources.

*Water geyser:* Erupts water, clean or polluted, in large steady amounts. Tamed and boxed, a water geyser feeds the colony's water line forever.

*Oil reservoir:* The oil biome's oil source — effectively endless, but it will not give up its oil on its own: it needs an oil well and a supply of water.

*Oil well:* Drills an oil reservoir, pumping water in and crude oil out, with a little natural gas as a byproduct. It turns a water surplus into fuel — and can empty a water reserve just as easily.

*Petroleum:* Crude oil refined into a better fuel at the oil refinery (or produced by a hot slickster). It runs the petroleum generator and is the raw material for plastic.

*Plastic:* Made from petroleum at the polymer press. It is the keystone mid-game material — required for the steam turbine and transit tubes — and it melts in heat, so keep your stockpile cool.

=== Automation

*Automation wire:* A second kind of wire that carries decisions instead of power — a green (on) or red (off) signal between buildings. It is how a sensor tells a machine what to do.

*Sensors:* Buildings that detect a condition — what a pipe is carrying, the depth of liquid in a cell, gas pressure, temperature — and output a green signal when their condition is met.

*Shutoffs:* Valves with an automation port that open when their wire is green and close when it is red. A shutoff does not pump; it gates whatever the pump already pushes.

*Logic gates:* Circuits that modify signals. The NOT gate flips green to red and back; AND and OR combine inputs. The smart battery paired with a generator through a shutoff is the classic first loop.

=== Spaced Out

*Rocket:* The Spaced Out vehicle that carries dupes and cargo between the asteroids of the cluster. Rockets are the endgame of travel — the reason the base gets fast and wide.

*Rocket platform:* The pad a rocket is built on and launches from. Each platform holds one rocket, and rockets need to be refueled and maintained on their pad between flights.

*Cargo modules:* The payload bays of a rocket that carry materials — and, in the passenger module, dupes — from one planetoid to another. They are how a colony moves its surplus to where it is needed.

*Planetoid:* The small asteroids that make up a Spaced Out cluster. Each has its own biomes, resources, and hazards, and some need a rocket to reach at all.

*Radiation:* The Spaced Out hazard that comes from space and radioactive materials. It damages unshielded dupes over time, but it also enables the radbolt economy.

*Radbolt:* A concentrated charge of radiation, collected by the radbolt collector and fired at research buildings. Radbolts are the currency of Spaced Out's advanced research.

*Bionic dupe:* The Bionic Booster Pack's robot dupe. Instead of food and oxygen, it runs on battery charge and has its own mentality mechanic — a dupe with different needs, and a different kind of trouble when those needs are ignored.
